We delve into how fundamental forcing methods can be employed to create $P$-points when attempting to validate the consistency of $2^{
\aleph_1}$ exceeding the highest possible value for $\aleph_1$. This approach involves the concept of a club filter, allowing us to generate $P$-points when certain cardinal inequalities are sought but prove unattainable. Specifically, when the target is a strictly increasing function from $\aleph_1$ to $\aleph_1$, our method yields a $P$-point for the case where the product of $2^{<\aleph_1}$ and $\aleph_1$ equals the cardinality of $\aleph_1$, whereas it fails for the scenario where the product of $2^{<\aleph_1}$ and $\aleph_1$ exceeds the cardinality of $\aleph_1$. Additionally, our work reveals that the approach based on side-by-side forcing cannot succeed in making $2^{
\aleph_1}$ exceeding the highest possible value for $\aleph_1$ consistent.
